%=====================================================================
\begin{abstract}
Transformer backbones such as Restormer have become the standard for
high-resolution image restoration, but they operate purely on low-level
appearance statistics and carry no explicit notion of \emph{what} is being
restored. We present the Semantic Rectified Restoration Transformer (SRRT), a
modular framework that augments a Restormer backbone with a semantic latent
pathway: a visual context encoder (SeeCoder), a damage-aware encoder (DiffAE),
a latent consistency regularizer (LCR), a rectified-flow latent transport, and
a semantic refinement attention block (SRA). Every module joins the backbone
through a \emph{zero-initialized additive gate} at the bottleneck, so the
untrained SRRT is bitwise identical to the backbone it extends. This makes the
ablation ladder honest by construction: any measured difference is attributable
to what a module learned, not to a perturbation it introduced at
initialization. We further show that a zero-output equivalence test is
\emph{insufficient} on its own---a gated module and a module wired to nothing
both produce identical output---and we introduce a complementary gradient-flow
connectivity test that distinguishes them. This test caught a genuine defect in
our own implementation in which a module was instantiated, consumed compute,
and never received gradient. We report a fully converged Restormer baseline
(28.937~dB PSNR, 0.8661 SSIM, 0.1356 LPIPS on 1111 GoPro test crops) and two
further admissible configurations, neither separating from that baseline by more
than 0.08~dB. We additionally diagnose a catastrophic divergence in the
rectified-flow configuration and trace it to two independent integration
defects: a regression target left attached to the shared restoration encoder,
which let the flow objective be minimized by degrading that encoder; and a
train/inference latent mismatch that taught the model to rely on an encoding of
the ground truth unavailable at test time. Each was isolated by a
single-variable experiment, and correcting both removes the divergence.
\textbf{No SRRT module is shown to improve restoration. This paper contributes
the framework, the validation methodology, a converged baseline and a failure
analysis---not a positive result.}
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
image restoration, motion deblurring, vision transformer, semantic guidance,
rectified flow, ablation methodology, reproducibility
\end{IEEEkeywords}

%=====================================================================
\section{Introduction}

Single-image motion deblurring is the task of recovering a sharp image
$\hat{x}$ from a blurred observation $y$. Modern approaches are dominated by
transformer architectures---Restormer~\cite{restormer}, SwinIR~\cite{swinir},
Uformer~\cite{uformer}---which model long-range dependencies far more
effectively than convolutional predecessors while remaining tractable at high
resolution.

These architectures share a limitation. They are trained end-to-end on pixel
and perceptual reconstruction objectives, and therefore learn a mapping driven
entirely by low-level appearance statistics. The network has no explicit
representation of scene content, and no explicit representation of \emph{how}
the image was degraded. When blur is severe, the reconstruction problem is
ill-posed: many sharp images are consistent with the observation, and a purely
appearance-driven prior has little basis for choosing among them.

This paper investigates whether an explicit semantic latent pathway helps. We
propose the Semantic Rectified Restoration Transformer (SRRT), which augments a
Restormer backbone with five modules operating on the bottleneck latent, and we
design the study so that the contribution of each module can be measured
independently.

Our contributions are:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{A modular semantic restoration architecture.} SRRT couples a
Restormer backbone to a semantic pathway comprising a visual context encoder, a
damage-aware encoder, a latent consistency regularizer, a rectified-flow
latent transport, and a semantic refinement attention block
(Section~\ref{sec:method}).

\item \textbf{Zero-gated integration.} Every module joins through a
zero-initialized additive gate, making untrained SRRT \emph{bitwise identical}
to Restormer. We verify this empirically for all six configurations
(Section~\ref{sec:zerogate}).

\item \textbf{A connectivity test that zero-output equivalence cannot replace.}
We show that identical output at initialization is a necessary but not
sufficient condition for correct integration, and give a gradient-flow test
that separates a correctly gated module from a disconnected one. This test
caught a real defect in our implementation (Section~\ref{sec:connectivity}).

\item \textbf{A converged baseline and an explicit, honest experimental
record.} We report the baseline in full, mark every unmeasured cell, and state
the protocol limitations that bound what the eventual results can claim
(Sections~\ref{sec:results} and~\ref{sec:limitations}).
\end{enumerate}

%=====================================================================
\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Transformer-based image restoration}
Restormer~\cite{restormer} makes high-resolution restoration tractable by
replacing spatial self-attention with \emph{multi-Dconv head transposed
attention} (MDTA), which computes attention across the \emph{channel}
dimension. For a feature map of $H\!\times\!W$ pixels and $C$ channels this
reduces cost from $\mathcal{O}((HW)^2 C)$ to $\mathcal{O}(HW\,C^2)$, i.e.\
linear rather than quadratic in spatial size. SwinIR~\cite{swinir} and
Uformer~\cite{uformer} instead constrain attention to shifted local windows.
NAFNet~\cite{nafnet} shows that much of the gain is attributable to
architectural simplification rather than attention itself.

\subsection{Semantic guidance for restoration}
Semantic priors have been used to condition restoration, typically through
segmentation maps or CLIP-style embeddings. SeeCoder~\cite{seecoder}, developed
for prompt-free diffusion, encodes an image into a dense visual context
representation intended to substitute for a text prompt. We adopt it as a
source of content-level context for the restoration latent.

\subsection{Learned degradation representations}
Diffusion autoencoders~\cite{diffae} learn a semantically meaningful,
decodable latent. We use the same encoder design not to generate, but to
produce a \emph{damage-aware} representation: a latent that encodes properties
of the degradation rather than of the clean content. We stress that no
disentanglement is guaranteed; the module is a learned representation and
nothing more.

\subsection{Flow-based transport}
Rectified flow~\cite{rectifiedflow} learns a velocity field transporting one
distribution to another along near-straight paths, permitting few-step
integration. We apply it in latent space, transporting a degraded-image latent
toward a clean-image latent, rather than in pixel space.

%=====================================================================
\section{Proposed Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Overview}
SRRT keeps the Restormer encoder--bottleneck--decoder path intact and adds a
parallel semantic pathway that meets the backbone at the \emph{bottleneck}. The
bottleneck is the natural junction: it is the lowest-resolution, most semantic
tensor in the network ($8C$ channels at $H/8 \times W/8$), so latent transport
is cheap there, and the decoder still has three upsampling stages in which to
propagate whatever the latent contributes.

Given a degraded input $y \in \mathbb{R}^{3\times H\times W}$, the backbone
encoder produces skip features and a bottleneck latent
$\ell \in \mathbb{R}^{8C \times H/8 \times W/8}$. The semantic pathway produces
a latent $z$ which is injected as

\begin{equation}
\ell' = \ell + \gamma \cdot W_{\text{inj}}(z),
\label{eq:inject}
\end{equation}

where $W_{\text{inj}}$ is a $1\!\times\!1$ convolution and $\gamma$ is a scalar
gate initialized to zero. The decoder then operates on $\ell'$.

\subsection{Backbone}
We use Restormer~\cite{restormer} with $C=48$, a four-level encoder, a
three-level decoder with pixel-shuffle resampling, and a refinement stage. Each
block pairs MDTA with a gated-Dconv feed-forward network (GDFN). MDTA computes

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Attn}(Q,K,V) = V \cdot \mathrm{softmax}\!\left(
\frac{\hat{Q}\hat{K}^{\top}}{\tau}\right),
\label{eq:mdta}
\end{equation}

where $\hat{Q},\hat{K}$ are $\ell_2$-normalized along the spatial axis and the
product $\hat{Q}\hat{K}^{\top}$ is $d\!\times\!d$ over channels---never
$HW\!\times\!HW$. The network predicts a global residual, $\hat{x} = f(y) + y$.

\subsection{Semantic modules}

\textbf{SeeCoder} encodes $y$ into a dense visual context tensor at
$H/8\times W/8$ with embedding width 256, using a lightweight multi-scale
encoder followed by self-attention blocks.

\textbf{DiffAE} encodes $y$ into a spatial damage latent at $H/8\times W/8$ and
a globally pooled semantic code, intended to capture degradation
characteristics.

\textbf{LCR (Latent Consistency Regularizer)} fuses the visual and damage
latents into an initial restoration latent $z_0$, and imposes a consistency
objective against a reference latent $z_{\text{ref}}$ obtained by encoding the
ground truth. Both are mapped through a \emph{shared} projection and
$\ell_2$-normalized:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{cons}} = \left\|
\frac{g(z_0)}{\|g(z_0)\|_2} -
\frac{g(\mathrm{sg}[z_{\text{ref}}])}{\|g(\mathrm{sg}[z_{\text{ref}}])\|_2}
\right\|_2^2 ,
\label{eq:lcr}
\end{equation}

where $\mathrm{sg}[\cdot]$ is a stop-gradient. Normalization makes the
objective scale-invariant, so it cannot be trivially minimized by shrinking
latent norms; the stop-gradient prevents the reference from collapsing toward
the prediction.

\textbf{Rectified flow} transports $z_0$ toward the clean-image latent $z_1$.
With $z_t = (1-t)z_0 + t z_1$ and target velocity $v^\star = z_1 - z_0$,

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{flow}} = \mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)}
\left\| v_\theta(z_t, t) - (z_1 - z_0) \right\|_2^2 .
\label{eq:flow}
\end{equation}

At inference $z_1$ is unavailable, so $\hat{z}_1$ is recovered by Euler
integration of $v_\theta$ from $z_0$.

\textbf{SRA (Semantic Refinement Attention)} applies a gated spatial attention
map $A_s$ to the refined decoder features:
$F' = F + \gamma_{\text{SRA}} \cdot A_s \odot R(F)$.

\subsection{Zero-gated integration}
\label{sec:zerogatemethod}
Each module enters through a gate initialized to exactly zero: $\gamma=0$ in
\eqref{eq:inject}, $\gamma_{\text{SRA}}=0$, and the rectified-flow output
projection is zero-initialized so $v_\theta \equiv 0$ at initialization, making
the ODE the identity map.

The consequence is that at initialization, for every configuration,
$\mathrm{SRRT}(y) = \mathrm{Restormer}(y)$ \emph{bitwise}. This is a
methodological device, not an implementation detail: it guarantees that all
rungs of the ablation ladder begin from the same function, so a measured
difference cannot be an artifact of initialization noise injected by the added
module.

Gradient still flows to $\gamma$ itself at the first step
($\partial \mathcal{L}/\partial \gamma = \langle \partial \mathcal{L}/\partial
\ell',\, W_{\text{inj}}(z)\rangle \neq 0$), so the gate opens and the module
begins to train from the second step onward.

\subsection{Training objective}
The total loss is

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} = \lambda_1 \mathcal{L}_{1}
            + \lambda_p \mathcal{L}_{\text{perc}}
            + \lambda_f \mathcal{L}_{\text{flow}}
            + \lambda_c \mathcal{L}_{\text{cons}},
\label{eq:total}
\end{equation}

with $\lambda_1=1.0$, $\lambda_p=0.1$ (VGG19 features~\cite{vgg}),
$\lambda_f=0.1$, $\lambda_c=0.1$. The flow and consistency terms are included
\emph{only} when their modules are enabled, so each rung of the ladder is
trained under exactly the objective its architecture implies.

%=====================================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\subsection{Dataset}
We use GoPro~\cite{gopro}: 2103 blurred/sharp training pairs and 1111 test
pairs. Images are normalized to $[0,1]$. Training uses $128\times128$ random
crops with augmentation; evaluation uses $256\times256$ center crops with no
augmentation.

\subsection{Training configuration}
AdamW~\cite{adamw} ($\eta=2\times10^{-4}$, weight decay $10^{-4}$,
$\beta=(0.9,0.999)$), batch size 2 with gradient accumulation 4 (effective
batch 8), gradient clipping at 1.0, and cosine annealing~\cite{sgdr} with
$T_{\max}$ equal to the epoch count and $\eta_{\min}=10^{-6}$. Mixed-precision
training~\cite{amp} is enabled. Seed 42. All runs execute on a single NVIDIA
RTX 4070 Laptop GPU (8~GB).

Training length is 60 epochs, i.e.\ 1051 iterations per epoch and 15{,}720
optimizer steps. Because $T_{\max}$ is tied to the epoch count, the learning
rate reaches $\eta_{\min}$ exactly when training ends; a schedule sized for a
different horizon would leave the model stopping mid-descent.

\subsection{Metrics}
PSNR, SSIM~\cite{ssim} and LPIPS~\cite{lpips} (AlexNet backbone). PSNR and SSIM
are higher-better; LPIPS is lower-better.

\subsection{Ablation design}
Six configurations form a cumulative ladder (Table~\ref{tab:ladder}). Each
differs from its predecessor by exactly one module, and all share the identical
dataset, protocol, optimizer, schedule and seed policy.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Ablation ladder. Each rung adds exactly one module.}
\label{tab:ladder}
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccccc@{}}
\toprule
& Configuration & SeeC. & DiffAE & LCR & Flow & SRA \\
\midrule
A & \texttt{baseline\_restormer} & -- & -- & -- & -- & -- \\
B & \texttt{seecoder}            & \checkmark & -- & -- & -- & -- \\
C & \texttt{seecoder\_diffae}    & \checkmark & \checkmark & -- & -- & -- \\
D & \texttt{..\_lcr}             & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & -- & -- \\
E & \texttt{..\_lcr\_flow}       & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & -- \\
F & \texttt{full\_srrt}          & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Validation methodology}
\label{sec:connectivity}
Before any configuration is trained we require two independent checks.

\textbf{Zero-gate equivalence.} The output of each configuration at
initialization must equal that of the bare backbone, given shared backbone
weights.

\textbf{Connectivity.} Every enabled module must receive non-zero gradient from
the \emph{full} training objective after the gate has opened.

The second check is not redundant. A module behind a zero gate and a module
that is wired to nothing at all produce \emph{identical} output at
initialization, and both pass the first check with an error of exactly zero.
They differ only in what happens after the first optimizer step. In our own
implementation, the SeeCoder pathway was initially constructed only when a
downstream module was also enabled; the SeeCoder-only configuration therefore
instantiated 335{,}380 parameters, consumed compute on every forward pass, and
never received gradient. Perturbing every SeeCoder weight by
$\mathcal{N}(0,10)$ changed the output by exactly $0.0$, and $0/44$ SeeCoder
tensors had a gradient. The zero-gate check reported a perfect pass throughout.
We consider this a general methodological point: \emph{equivalence tests
validate initialization, not integration.}

Additionally, the connectivity test must score the full objective. With
rectified flow enabled the backbone is fed the supervised endpoint $z_1$ during
training, so the SeeCoder$\rightarrow$LCR$\rightarrow z_0$ path reaches the loss
through $\mathcal{L}_{\text{flow}}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\text{cons}}$ rather than
through the pixel term; a test scoring only the pixel loss would report those
modules as disconnected.

