\end{table}

\subsubsection{Admissibility}
\label{sec:admissibility}
A run enters Table~\ref{tab:ablation} only if it completed its full 60-epoch
cosine schedule with the learning rate reaching $\eta_{\min}$. Three runs fail
this test and are excluded:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{B} stopped at epoch 40 of 60 with the learning rate still at
$4.63\times10^{-5}$. Its measured 28.496~dB is therefore the score of a model
that trained 19 fewer epochs and never annealed. Reporting it beside the fully
annealed baseline would invite the false reading that SeeCoder costs 0.44~dB,
when the deficit is confounded with training length.
\item \textbf{E} and \textbf{F} diverged (Section~\ref{sec:flowfailure}). Their
best checkpoints are epochs 5 and 6 of 60.
\end{itemize}

Among admissible rows, C is statistically indistinguishable from the baseline
($-0.001$~dB) and D is $+0.077$~dB above it. Both differences are far smaller
than the unmeasured run-to-run variance discussed in
Section~\ref{sec:limitations}, and we therefore claim no improvement from any
module. The correct reading of Table~\ref{tab:ablation} is that no SRRT
component has yet been shown to help.

\textbf{Statistical protocol.} Because arms are evaluated on the identical image
set, differences are assessed \emph{pairwise per image}: mean, median, standard
deviation, a 95\% confidence interval on the mean difference, win/loss counts
and a Wilcoxon signed-rank test. Such an interval quantifies sampling over
\emph{test images} for one fixed pair of checkpoints. It is not a training-run
variance estimate, and with a single run per arm a statistically significant
per-image difference may still reflect seed noise.

\subsection{Failure analysis of the rectified-flow integration}
\label{sec:flowfailure}

Configurations E and F did not merely score poorly: they diverged. E fell from
24.098~dB at epoch~5 to 14.527~dB at epoch~6 and reached 6.606~dB by epoch~9,
while D---identical but for the flow module---climbed monotonically past 24.6~dB
over the same epochs. Diagnosis identified two independent defects, each
isolated by a single-variable 10-epoch experiment.

\textbf{Defect 1: the regression target was trainable.} The velocity target
$v^\star = z_1 - z_0$ was formed with $z_1$ still attached to the graph. Because
$z_1$ is produced by the \emph{shared restoration encoder},
$\mathcal{L}_{\text{flow}}$ could be reduced by moving $z_1$ toward $z_0$---that
is, by degrading the encoder the restoration path depends on---which is an
easier descent direction than learning $v_\theta$. Measured gradient norms
confirm this: $\mathcal{L}_{\text{flow}}$ deposited \emph{more} gradient on the
encoder ($4.23\times10^{-2}$) than on the velocity network it was meant to train
($1.97\times10^{-2}$). The signature is diagnostic: over the failed run
$\mathcal{L}_{\text{flow}}$ fell $31\times$ while validation PSNR fell 10~dB.
The objective was being satisfied by destroying the encoder. Detaching $z_1$
drives encoder gradient to exactly zero and removes the collapse.

\textbf{Defect 2: train/inference latent mismatch.} With the collapse removed, a
second pathology dominated: training injected $z_1$---an encoding of the ground
truth---into the bottleneck, while inference injects $\mathrm{integrate}(z_0)$.
The model learned to rely on information unavailable at test time. The gate grew
$318\times$ in ten epochs, training loss fell from 0.223 to 0.169 while
validation loss \emph{rose} from 0.241 to 0.298, and on the resulting checkpoint
the same weights scored 24.819~dB with $z_1$ injected against 18.946~dB with the
ODE latent---a 5.874~dB train/inference gap. Injecting $z_0$ during training, so
that the injected tensor derives only from the degraded input, closes this gap
to $0.000$~dB.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Rectified-flow diagnostics, 10 epochs each, identical to E in every
hyperparameter. Each row changes exactly one thing relative to the row above.}
\label{tab:flowdiag}
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Configuration & PSNR ep.\ 6 & PSNR ep.\ 9 & GT/ODE gap \\
\midrule
E, as published            & 14.527 & 6.606  & --- \\
+ detached $z_1$           & 22.846 & 20.250 & 5.874 dB \\
+ inject $z_0$             & \textbf{24.263} & \textbf{24.596} & \textbf{0.000 dB} \\
\midrule
D (+LCR), no flow          & 24.250 & 24.625 & n/a \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:flowdiag} summarises the two interventions. With both applied the
flow configuration tracks D to within 0.03~dB at epoch~9 and shows no collapse
or divergence. We stress what this does \emph{not} establish: at that point
$\gamma \approx 1.8\times10^{-3}$, so the flow branch contributes almost nothing
to the output. The corrected configuration is demonstrably no longer
\emph{harmful}; whether it \emph{helps} requires the full 60-epoch run, which
had not completed at the time of writing. E and F must be retrained before
either can appear in Table~\ref{tab:ablation}.

%=====================================================================
\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

We state the limitations that bound what this study can conclude, including
those not yet resolved.

\textbf{Validation and test are the same split.} The validation loader reads
the GoPro test split, so best-checkpoint selection and reported metrics use the
same 1111 images. There is no held-out third split. Reported figures are
therefore validation numbers and may be mildly optimistic through model
selection. This is a limitation of the present protocol and should be corrected
by carving a validation subset from the training split.

\textbf{Run-to-run variance is unmeasured.} Each configuration is trained once.
Without a seed-repeat study, no threshold exists below which a difference is
indistinguishable from training noise. We regard a seed-repeat of the baseline
as a prerequisite for interpreting small effects.

\textbf{Training budget.} At 15{,}720 optimizer steps and a fixed $128$~px crop,
all configurations are far from the published Restormer training budget. Module
benefits that require longer training, or larger crops, would not appear here.

\textbf{Train/inference asymmetry in the flow path.} During training the
backbone is injected with the supervised endpoint $z_1$; at inference it
receives the ODE solution $\hat{z}_1$. This exposure bias is unaddressed.

\textbf{Untrained sub-block.} The DiffAE semantic head (131{,}584 parameters)
is computed on every forward pass but does not enter the objective; it is
surfaced only for visualization. It inflates the parameter counts of
configurations C--F and should be either removed or given a loss before those
configurations are compared.

\textbf{Scope.} Only GoPro motion deblurring is evaluated. Loss weights are
defaults and untuned.

%=====================================================================
\section{Conclusion}

We presented SRRT, a modular framework that attaches a semantic latent pathway
to a Restormer backbone through zero-initialized gates, together with the
validation methodology needed to make its ablation study trustworthy. We
reported a converged baseline, verified bitwise zero-gate equivalence for all
six configurations, and accounted for the parameter cost of each.

Our main transferable findings are methodological, and both concern ways an
ablation study can silently measure the wrong thing.

First, \emph{a zero-output equivalence test is insufficient to establish that a
module is integrated}. A correctly gated module and a disconnected one are
indistinguishable under such a test, and only a gradient-flow check separates
them. This is not hypothetical: it occurred in our implementation and was
invisible to an equivalence test reporting a perfect pass.

Second, \emph{an auxiliary objective whose regression target is produced by a
shared trainable component has a degenerate solution}. Our flow loss could be
minimized by corrupting the restoration encoder rather than by learning the
velocity field, and it preferentially did so---depositing more gradient on the
encoder than on the network it was meant to train. The loss fell $31\times$
while the metric it was supposed to help fell 10~dB. Any auxiliary loss of this
shape should detach its target, and a falling auxiliary loss alongside a falling
task metric should be treated as a symptom rather than a curiosity.

The question this framework was built to answer---whether semantic latent
guidance improves transformer-based deblurring, and which module is
responsible---remains open, and the evidence so far is not encouraging: the two
admissible SRRT configurations differ from the baseline by at most 0.08~dB,
which is well inside the unmeasured run-to-run variance. Establishing whether
any module helps requires the corrected flow runs, a seed-repeat study to
calibrate that variance, and a training budget closer to the literature's. We
will report those outcomes, including negative ones, under the statistical
protocol of Section~\ref{sec:ablationresults}.

